            <ArticleTitle>Parental instrumental feeding, negative affect, and binge eating among overweight individuals.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>107-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.eatbeh.2015.01.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1471-0153(15)00015-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Parental instrumental feeding (i.e., rewarding children with food for perceived correct behaviors and punishing by taking away food for perceived incorrect behaviors) and negative affect are independently associated with binge eating in adulthood. However, less is known about interactions between these variables and binge eating. This study examined the relationship of retrospective reports of parental feeding practices and negative affect to binge eating. Participants were 165 overweight and obese undergraduate students at a large Mid-Atlantic University. High parental instrumental feeding strengthened the relationship between negative affect and binge eating. Also, individuals who reported low parental feeding practices reported similar binge eating regardless of negative affect. These findings suggest that overweight and obese individuals whose parents used more instrumental feeding practices are most likely to engage in binge eating in response to negative affect.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
